<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">People's United Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1950">1950</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The People's United Party is a political party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a>, founded in 1950 in protest at a currency devaluation in British Honduras. Led by George Price, it campaigned for self-government and independence, won it in 1981 and has since alternated in power with the United Democratic Party.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peoples-united-party.jpg" alt="People's United Party" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fry1989 eh? · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Belize_People%27s_United_Party.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In the 1940s politics in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">British Honduras</a> was led by trade unionists of the Workers and Tradesmen's Union, later the General Workers Union. A group of college-educated men, among them John Smith, George Price, Herbert Fuller and Herman Jex, won seats on the Belize Town Board and later the Legislative Council. On 31 December 1949 the governor devalued the British Honduras dollar after saying he would not, and in January 1950 they formed the People's Committee to speak out against the devaluation and colonial rule.</p><p>On 12 February 1950 a crowd of 10,000 marched to Government House in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize-city">Belize City</a>; police used tear gas on a meeting at Battlefield Park, and a state of emergency lasted until July. In September the committee concluded that its work needed a political party, and on 29 September 1950 it dissolved itself and the People's United Party was born. In 1951 the colonial government dissolved the PUP-dominated Belize City Council and jailed the leaders Leigh Richardson and Phillip Goldson for an article justifying revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Price became head of the General Workers Union in 1952, the year of a 49-day strike that greatly raised support for the union and the party. At the first general elections, on 28 April 1954, the PUP won eight of nine seats. In 1956 Richardson, Goldson and other central committee members left, and Price led the party unchallenged for nearly four decades; in 1961 it took all eighteen seats. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-hattie">Hurricane Hattie</a> struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize-city">Belize City</a> in October 1961 it called for a capital on higher ground, which led to the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belmopan">Belmopan</a>.</p><p>In 1981 Price brought back the Heads of Agreement, proposals meant to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a>'s claim to the country; protests forced a state of emergency, under which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">Belize</a> became independent on 21 September 1981. The PUP lost the December 1984 election to the United Democratic Party by 21 seats to 7, returned under Price in 1989 and lost again in 1993.</p><p>Price resigned as leader in 1996, and under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/said-musa">Said Musa</a> the party won a landslide in 1998 and 22 of 29 seats in 2003. It lost heavily in February 2008, winning six of 31 seats, stayed in opposition after the elections of 2012 and 2015, and won in 2020, holding 26 seats in 2025.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “People's United Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>British Honduras was renamed Belize in 1973, and despite Guatemala's claim to the territory it became independent on 21 September 1981 under George Price of the <b>People's United Party</b>, with the British monarch as head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/belize">Belize</a></li><li><blockquote>Currency devaluation in 1949 sparked the nationalist <b>People's United Party</b>, led by George Price.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-honduras">British Honduras</a></li></ul></section>
